WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.970
Film Courage: …And how do we do this faster,
so most people would probably turn to education

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:11.320
as another form of hacking, how do you get
past a certain time frame of putting energy

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.650
into something?

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:17.360
Is formal education totally necessary when
it comes to writing?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:24.120
Can’t someone still be a brilliant screenwriter
without being part of an MFA program or even

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.000
obtain a Bachelor’s degree in writing?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.529
Eric Edson: The answer is simple…And the
answer is absolutely “Yes.

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:33.660
Of course they can.”

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.880
They can.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:44.610
But…that for most of us is choosing a very
difficult and lonely road.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.820
And here is why.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:57.390
Any art form…before anything can be art
and be appreciated as anything that has reached

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:02.010
to the level of art, it is a craft.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:08.789
First art is created by craftspeople who have
some inspiration at one moment in their lives

00:01:08.789 --> 00:01:15.159
or 50 moments in their lives when the craft
that they do so well, rises to the level of

00:01:15.159 --> 00:01:16.189
art.

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:24.170
But you cannot cut to art without swimming
through craft, without creating an understanding

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:28.709
the craft of anything.

00:01:28.709 --> 00:01:34.579
Georgia O'Keeffe (I don’t know why she came
to mind) and then Pablo Picasso, okay graphic

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:39.159
artists who take it to the level of true and
pure art.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:44.289
Both of them in their early years learned
their craft.

00:01:44.289 --> 00:01:50.099
They learned perception, they learned color,
composition.

00:01:50.099 --> 00:01:55.149
There are things you have to learn, tools
that these artists have and when they are

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:59.959
mastered, then they can take it into some
very interesting and unexpected places.

00:01:59.959 --> 00:02:04.179
But you’ve still got the tools.

00:02:04.179 --> 00:02:08.920
Chess is a rather interesting game and there
are untold millions of possibilities, maybe

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:10.789
infinite possibilities.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:13.579
There are six pieces in a chess game.

00:02:13.579 --> 00:02:21.680
But how to use those six pieces and multiple
combinations create probably the most fascinating,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:27.340
intricate and mind twisting game ever invented.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:34.640
There are tools in screenwriting and I would
take the position that if this is what somebody

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.629
really wants, if this is what they’re going
for, if all their gifts lead them in this

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:45.989
direction, then they need to get an education
about the tools.

00:02:45.989 --> 00:02:48.340
You can do that on your own.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:53.689
But you’ll need to read a lot of books and
sometimes…I mean there are a lot of great

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:55.510
books on screenwriting, don’t get me wrong.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.810
There are a lot of great books.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:03.239
And there are tons and tons of garbage books
on screenwriting, too.

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:09.530
And if you haven’t got some guidance, you
may be wasting a lot of time on some of those

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.770
books.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:16.540
But if you come to an MFA program you are
pursuing a couple of things really.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.959
And what do you get for your money, you know.

00:03:19.959 --> 00:03:26.530
First of all, you are going toward a terminal
degree, a graduate, terminal degree that qualifies

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:36.629
you to teach tenure track as a professor at
the college and or university level.

00:03:36.629 --> 00:03:40.569
And that’s a major certificate, that’s
a major thing to have.

00:03:40.569 --> 00:03:44.450
Like me, I had two of them stashed away in
the closet somewhere when the opportunity

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.230
came up decades later.

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:47.230
Okay!

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.230
I’m qualified.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:49.480
I’m prepared, you know?

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.499
Let’s go.

00:03:50.499 --> 00:03:55.650
So that as a writer it gives you another door
to open.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.359
Another avenue to pursue in life.

00:03:58.359 --> 00:03:59.359
You are less hindered.

00:03:59.359 --> 00:04:05.459
Remember I said you have two pathways to be
pursued at the same time, your craft and art,

00:04:05.459 --> 00:04:06.459
yes?

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:10.359
But at the same time how to regularize an
income.

00:04:10.359 --> 00:04:16.470
Well you’re a big step toward the second
path when you have an MFA, just the practicality

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:17.470
of the degree.

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:24.220
But on the other set of accomplishments, it’s
about the tools.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.990
Tools you will learn that you never knew existed!

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:35.380
So many people who don’t get a formal education
in it or a degree, they still talk of screenwriting

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:39.970
as something that falls from the sky “I
don’t want that stuff!

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:41.849
I don’t want to do plot!”

00:04:41.849 --> 00:04:45.720
I just read this some place and it made me
shake my head.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:45.950
Somebody said…

